caramos-ota (1.5.1-0caramos1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Computers that already had an /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list, made by their user
    or by another program, could not update to 1.4.0 or 1.5.0 (ticket
    H-1207). 1.4.0 shipped the office defaults as that configuration file;
    dpkg asked which one to keep, the update has no terminal, dpkg read
    end-of-file, caramos-ota stayed unconfigured and the update stopped with
    "OTA self-update failed", every day again. The self-update runs the
    installed caramos-ota, so the package itself no longer has that file:
    the same defaults are /usr/share/applications/x-cinnamon-mimeapps.list
    and xfce-mimeapps.list, the system's default apps of each edition's
    desktop. A user's own choice and an administrator's
    /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list come first, as they should.
  * Such a computer repairs itself with this version: the next daily check
    or update installs it without a question, and the update goes on. The
    user's /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list stays as it is; the copy of the old list
    dpkg left next to it (mimeapps.list.dpkg-new) is deleted.
  * On computers with 1.4.0 or 1.5.0, /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list is deleted when
    it is still the one caramos-ota installed; a file someone changed stays
    as it is (preinst, postinst and postrm; no rename to .dpkg-bak).
  * dpkg asks no more questions in updates: the self-update, the repair, the
    rollback, migrations, Driver Check's Canon driver and Phòng máy run
    apt-get and dpkg with --force-confdef --force-confold and
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive, as automatic updates did. A
    configuration file someone changed stays as it is.
  * Passwords no longer show while they are typed (ticket H-1200): with
    Vietnamese typing on, Cinnamon's password boxes (installing updates or
    software, unlocking) showed what was typed before it turned into dots.
    A patch tells Fcitx that those boxes are passwords (migration
    20261123120000_hidden_password_typing; upstream Cinnamon fixed it in
    6.8).
  * Copying files on the Xfce desktop and pasting them in Files works
    (ticket H-1204): xfdesktop ends each line of the copied list with \r\n,
    and Files looked for names ending in \r. The Xfce Control Center
    rewrites the clipboard's list without it.
  * Files' Open in Terminal works on the Xfce edition (ticket H-1185): it
    started GNOME Terminal, which only the Cinnamon edition has; it now
    starts xfce4-terminal there (60_caramos-ota.gschema.override).
  * Desktop shortcuts for Google Drive, network folders and phones (ticket
    H-1190): Create a desktop shortcut failed on them. The new
    caramos-desktop-shortcut makes them with the folder's name, also for
    names with #, and they open after a restart (migration
    20261123120100_desktop_shortcuts_anywhere).
  * Show desktop at the end of the taskbar on both editions, as on Windows
    (tickets H-1189, H-1192, H-1201, H-1202): clicking the right edge after
    the right island hides every window, and again brings them back.
    Cinnamon's corner bar applet, and on Xfce the new caramos-show-desktop
    plugin of xfce4-caramos-plugin 1.1.0 (migration
    20261124100000_show_desktop_button). Super+D shows the desktop and
    Super+E opens Files (Nemo) on the Xfce edition too (migration
    20261124100100_xfce_super_d_super_e).
  * PrtSc copies the whole screen to the clipboard on both editions, with a
    short notification, as on Windows 10 (tickets H-1188, H-1193):
    caramos-print-screen (migration
    20261124100200_print_screen_to_clipboard). Super+Shift+S still picks a
    part of the screen.
  * Xfce edition:
    - Appearance's Enable event sounds works (ticket H-1206), still off by
      default (migration 20261124100300_xfce_event_sounds);
    - the Control Center button shows a USB drive while one is plugged in,
      and its popup lists it with Safely remove (ticket H-1195);
    - pointing at the button says what the network icon shows (ticket
      H-1205);
    - the names under desktop icons have a dark shadow, which reads on
      light wallpapers (ticket H-1187).
  * Cinnamon edition: the same shadow under the names of desktop icons
    (ticket H-1187, migration 20261124120000_desktop_icon_labels), through a
    copy of Nemo's own stylesheet that caramos-nemo-menu writes.
  * fastfetch on both editions (ticket H-1203): migration
    20261124130000_install_fastfetch installs it from the CaramOS PPA
    (fastfetch 2.71.0-0caramos1, a backport for noble whose default config
    shows the CaramOS logo). New ISOs get it from the same migration.
    neofetch stays.
  * Tray icons follow Dark mode without logging out (ticket H-1211). After the
    Dark mode tile, the right island kept some icons in the old theme's
    colours, nearly invisible, until the next login: Update Manager's shield
    and System Information's mark (XAppStatusIcons shown as GtkStatusIcons:
    GTK draws them once in the colour the tray gives, and xfce4-panel's tray
    sets that colour only when it starts), CopyQ's scissors and Cinnamon's
    Flameshot flame (pictures drawn from the icon theme at start-up) and
    Lotus's V (an icon name Lotus picks by colour and looks at again only when
    a text field gets the focus). Once a theme or icon theme change has
    settled, the Control Center of each edition runs
    /usr/share/caramos-ota/control-center/caramos-tray-refresh: on Xfce it
    gives the tray the island's text colour, then every XAppStatusIcon makes a
    new icon (a name of XApp's status monitor namespace is owned and
    released), and after a new icon theme CopyQ (after "copyq exit", which
    saves its history) and a Flameshot in the old XEmbed tray start again.
    Migration 20261124170000_tray_icons_follow_theme links each of Lotus's
    names into Tela-circle-dark and Tela-circle-light with the picture in that
    theme's colour, so the V follows the icon theme at once.
  * Tray icons keep their place in Cinnamon's tray (H-1211 follow-up).
    The tray applet put every new icon first, so the order was the order in
    which programs started, and each Dark mode switch moved the icons the
    refresh above makes again (Update Manager's shield, System Information's
    mark, the Flameshot flame) to the front. Migration
    20261124180000_tray_icons_keep_their_place gives the applet a fixed
    order: other programs' icons first, by name, then System Information,
    Update Manager, CopyQ, the input method and Flameshot next to the clock,
    the order a CaramOS login shows. A Cinnamon update gets the change again
    (caramos_ota_update.cinnamon_patches).
  * The Update Center no longer says CaramOS is up to date without asking.
    Up to 1.5.0, opened by hand, it only read the updater already installed:
    where the daily check kept failing, it said "CaramOS is up to date,
    latest version 1.3.3" while 1.5.0 was out (CaramOS 1.3.3 computers of a
    company, 2026-11). It now runs that check first, through pkexec without
    a password (polkit action net.vietnamlinuxfamily.caramos-ota.check, the
    person at the computer only; /usr/share/caramos-ota/check/
    caramos-ota-check-helper). It shows what the check found, or why it
    could not check: the update server could not be reached, another update
    is running, or an error, with Try again and Ask for help. A check that
    installs a newer updater opens the window again with that updater's
    code. Not on a live USB or a frozen system drive.
  * A check that cannot reach the CaramOS PPA fails. apt-get update ends
    with 0 and only a warning when a source cannot be reached at all (a
    firewall, a name that does not resolve), and kept the old list: the
    check then found nothing new, every day, and said nothing.
  * A failed daily check runs again an hour later, until one works
    (caramos-ota-check.service: Restart=on-failure, RestartSec=1h); up to
    1.5.0 the next try was the next day's.
  * The Trash is on the desktop on both editions, as on Windows (ticket
    H-1210). Up to 1.5.0 CaramOS hid it: Cinnamon's first-login script set
    it off in every account, so migration
    20261124140000_show_trash_on_desktop turns it on once for each account
    and in the defaults and that script; on Xfce the default says on, and a
    user's own choice in Desktop Settings stays. A logged-in desktop shows
    it at once when the update comes from the Update Center; an automatic
    update waits until nobody is logged in.

